cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Caterva in academic or research work, please cite it as below.
  This file exists for the same reason the test suite checks every number
  against an exact solution: a claim should be traceable back to something
  concrete, and "cite this project" deserves the same treatment as "cite
  this Km value."
title: Caterva
abstract: >-
  A biology simulation engine built on antimony/SBML/roadrunner, providing
  validated enzyme kinetics (Michaelis-Menten), epidemiology (SIR/SEIR), and
  PCR amplification models with literature-grounded parameter plausibility
  checks.

  Caterva is NOT Tellurium. Tellurium is a separate, established
  systems-biology environment from the Sauro lab at the University of
  Washington and collaborators. Caterva is an unaffiliated personal
  project: not a fork of Tellurium, not endorsed by its authors, and
  making no claim to their work. Caterva is a consumer of that ecosystem,
  running on libRoadRunner (Apache 2.0, University of Washington) and
  generating Antimony (MIT). The project was called Terrium until 2026-09-27, too close to Tellurium's name; it has been Caterva since.
authors:
  - name: Caterva
    website: "https://github.com/math12345678/caterva"
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/math12345678/caterva"
# The version matches caterva.__version__ (which pyproject.toml reads) and
# package.json, kept equal to the release tag so "cite the version you
# used" resolves to a real tag. package.json said 1.0.0 until 2026-08-29;
# 0.1.0 was the source-only release of that date; 0.2.0 the first with
# built artifacts (2026-09-19); 0.3.0 the first on GitHub's Releases page
# with app folders (2026-09-21); 0.3.1 adds the user's guide (2026-09-22);
# 0.4.0 is the rename to Caterva (2026-09-27); 0.5.0 adds Caterva Studio and
# the enzyme finder; 0.5.1 adds updates inside Caterva.app.
version: 0.5.1
date-released: 2026-10-04
